#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# airy_bi

#% text
# `airy_bi(x)` gives the Airy function `Bi(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `Bi(x)` is the second solution of `y'' = x y` (DLMF 9.2), growing as `x` tends to `inf` and oscillating with the same amplitude as `Ai` for negative `x`.

#% item
# `airy_bi(0)` is `3^(-1/6)/gamma(2/3)`. Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `airy_bi` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
airy_bi(0)
#% output
#= 3^(5/6)/(3*gamma(2/3))

#% cell
airy_bi(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.2074235949528713

#% cell
airy_bi(-5.0)
#% output
#= -0.13836913490160058

#% cell
n(airy_bi(1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.2074235949528712594363788170282870

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Fast growth:

#% cell
airy_bi(10.0)
#% output
#= 455641153.54822516

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
airy_bi(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.7166580733827684 + 0.6198892904008447im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The Wronskian `Ai Bi' - Ai' Bi = 1/pi`:

#% cell
airy_ai(0.7)*airy_bi_prime(0.7) - airy_ai_prime(0.7)*airy_bi(0.7) - 1/pi
#% output
#= -2.7755575615628914e-17

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(airy_bi(x), x)
#% output
#= airy_bi_prime(x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `airy_ai`, `airy_bi_prime`, `airy_ai_prime`
